The two accepted phases currently account for the main peak positions, but their 23% Rwp is dominated by deliberately unrefined line-shape and specimen effects. I’m tightening those first; this is necessary before deciding whether the weak Pbcn family has a stable, nonzero scale or is merely residual structure from the main phases.

The staged profile fit is still running; GSAS-II is buffering its cycle output. The important safeguard here is that phase size and strain are being bounded individually, so a lower Rwp cannot be bought with negative crystallite size or strain—the failure mode seen in the initial exploratory fit.

Normalizing the COD references to the generator’s documented convention (neutral atoms, isotropic Uiso = 0.005 Å², symmetry restandardized) improved the two-phase fit and revealed an important basis change for WO₃. I’m now refining the heavy-atom and oxygen coordinates from those physically correct starts, one phase at a time, with displacement and bond checks after each stage.

The physically bounded nuisance refinement is converging slowly because GSAS-II is testing several damping levels. The accepted coordinate changes remain small (about 0.02–0.10 Å); the all-at-once WO₃ oxygen trial was rejected because it moved oxygens by 0.5–1.3 Å and worsened Rwp.

The first systematic Pbcn analogue search is too slow at its current resolution, so I’m cutting it down to a coarse lattice scan followed by refinement only for the best starts. The first inverse-columbite start already finds a plausible broad-profile match, which confirms that the Fe₂WO₆-derived ordering is not the only Pbcn model that needs a fair test.

The coarse family search now favors two Pbcn Mn₂WO₆ starts with the same adapted metric (roughly 4.63 × 17.7 × 5.17 Å): direct Fe₂WO₆ cation ordering and an inverse-columbite Ta₂ZnO₆ ordering. I’m testing both in the same refined two-phase project with identical scale, cell, and broadening stages; this is the decisive scale-collapse test requested by the closure rule.

Both Pbcn starts retain a small nonzero scale, but neither closes the residual reflection family: strong unmatched peaks remain near 20.41°, 28.64°, 32.47°, and 35.11°. I’m therefore treating Pbcn Mn₂WO₆ as provisional and expanding the topology search across ordered A₂BO₆ frameworks before accepting any third phase.

The broader screen is already finding several substantially better reflection-family matches than Pbcn, spanning monoclinic, tetragonal, and orthorhombic frameworks. I’m letting the full symmetry set finish, then I’ll promote only the leading chemically plausible, distinct topologies into equal-depth GSAS-II trials.

A repeated high-scoring family is emerging in tetragonal space group P4₂/mnm (No. 136), with a consistent adapted cell around a = 5.172 Å, c = 8.84 Å across chemically unrelated prototypes. That reproducibility is stronger evidence than any single database hit; I’m keeping monoclinic and orthorhombic leaders as controls until the screen completes.

